{
  "suite": "money.parse",
  "spec": "0.1.0",
  "description": "Human-facing decimal strings (spec 3.6): strict ASCII, excess precision rejected unless a rounding mode is given. `minor` is a decimal string so i64 extremes survive JavaScript numbers. Rounding modes are defined in spec 3.6 (down truncates toward zero).",
  "source": "Hand-derived from ISO 4217 exponents and spec 3.2/3.6; no implementation consulted. kes-down-negative: -1029.5 truncated toward zero is -1029 (a floor would give -1030). Trailing-zero cases (added after review, spec 3.6): KES has exponent 2, so \"10.290\" has 3 fractional digits, one more than allowed; reject therefore fails on the digit count alone, and with half_up the dropped digit 0 is below half so the result is 1029 (10.29 -> 1029 minor units); the negative case is rejected by the same count rule. Decimal-grammar edges (added after review): the decimal input grammar written in spec 3.6 (optional minus, digits, optional dot and digits; leading zeros permitted; negative zero forms parse to 0) is deliberately looser than the wire rule of 3.2, which governs the wire amount only, so \"007\" is the integer 7, i.e. 700 KES minor units, and \"-0.00\" is zero (an integer has no sign of zero), i.e. 0.",
